    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 3100 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-minoan-period">Early Minoan period</a> begins as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1900 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The first palaces are built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-malia">Malia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1700 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The first palaces are destroyed, probably by earthquakes, and rebuilt on a grander scale</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">17th or 16th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The eruption of Thera buries the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akrotiri-prehistoric-city">Akrotiri</a> under volcanic ash</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1450 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Fires destroy palaces and villas across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>; Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">14th or 13th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> is finally destroyed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1200–1100 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Cretans move to defensible heights such as Karphi as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> ends</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1900</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> begins to excavate the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/knossos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/knossos.jpg" alt="Knossos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_-_North_Portico_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, the largest of the Greek islands, was permanently settled around 7000 BCE by farmers who brought wheat, barley, sheep, goats, pigs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> across the sea, probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Their village at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> lasted for nearly four thousand years, leaving a deep mound of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> layers beneath the later palace. Around 3100 BCE, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and then bronze working spread, the island entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-minoan-period">Early Minoan period</a>. Villages grew, potters made fine wares such as the mottled Vasiliki ware of eastern Crete, and seafarers brought obsidian from the island of Melos. The dead were laid in communal tombs used for many generations: round stone tholos tombs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesara-plain">Mesara Plain</a> of the south and rectangular house tombs in the east, such as those of Mochlos, whose gold diadems and jewellery show that some families were already rich.</p><p>Around 1900 BCE, early in the Middle Minoan period, the first palaces were built at Knossos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-malia">Malia</a>: large complexes around a central court where an elite stored produce, organised workshops and kept written records. Why they arose is debated. Some scholars stress growing wealth and competition between leading families, others the stimulus of trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, where palaces had stood for centuries, and others the role of older open courts where communities gathered to feast and worship. Around 1700 BCE the first palaces were destroyed, probably by earthquakes, and rebuilt on a grander scale, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neopalatial-period">Neopalatial period</a>, the height of Minoan civilisation.</p><p>The names and periods used today are modern. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a>, who excavated Knossos from 1900, popularised the name “Minoan” after the mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a> and divided Cretan prehistory into Early, Middle and Late Minoan phases on the evidence of pottery styles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaos-platon">Nikolaos Platon</a> later proposed periods based on the palaces: Prepalatial, Protopalatial (c. 1900–1700 BCE), Neopalatial (c. 1700–1450 BCE) and Postpalatial. Both schemes are still used. Calendar dates rest on links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and on radiocarbon dating, and some of them remain disputed.</p><p>What the Minoans called themselves is unknown. Studies of ancient DNA published in 2013 and 2017 found that they descended mainly from the Neolithic farmers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and Anatolia, with some later ancestry related to ancient peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and that they were genetically close to the Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of the mainland.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/phaistos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phaistos.jpg" alt="Phaistos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Festos1(js).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jerzy Strzelecki, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The palaces were the hubs of Minoan society. Each was built around a long rectangular central court, surrounded by storerooms, workshops, shrines and halls rising to two or more storeys, and the largest faced paved west courts. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, the largest, long rows of magazines held great clay storage jars, pithoi, for oil, wine and grain. Light <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, columned porches, grand staircases and halls whose walls could be opened by rows of doors made the buildings airy, and stone drains and terracotta pipes carried water and waste.</p><p>Around the palaces lay towns of stone houses. Knossos was one of the largest settlements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, and smaller towns such as Gournia, excavated by the American archaeologist Harriet Boyd between 1901 and 1904, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaikastro">Palaikastro</a> show paved streets, workshops and small shrines. In the countryside, large houses often called villas, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-triada">Ayia Triada</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a> and Vathypetro south of Knossos, managed farmland, presses and storage. Harbour towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kommos">Kommos</a>, probably the port of Phaistos, linked the island to the sea.</p><p>The economy rested on grain, olives and vines, on flocks of sheep and goats that supplied wool for textiles, and on crafts and trade. The palaces collected and stored produce and supported specialist craftsmen, though much farming and exchange probably lay outside their control. Officials sealed jars, boxes and doors with lumps of clay stamped by seals and kept accounts, first in Cretan hieroglyphic and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a>. The largest archive of Linear A, some 150 tablets, comes from Ayia Triada. Although the language cannot be read, the numbers and the signs for goods can: the tablets record quantities of grain, figs, wine and oil.</p><p>Who ruled is unknown. Evans imagined a priest-king at Knossos and named a room with a stone seat the Throne Room, but in its final form that room belongs to the period of Mycenaean rule. Minoan art has no certain portraits of rulers and none of the scenes of royal victory common in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. Some scholars think each palace had its own ruling family, others that Knossos dominated the island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neopalatial-period">Neopalatial period</a>, and others that priestesses or councils of leading families held power. Women appear prominently in wall paintings and ritual scenes, which has prompted long debate about their status, but these are images of the elite and of ceremonies rather than of everyday life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bull-leaping"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bull-leaping.jpg" alt="Bull-leaping" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bull-leaping">Bull-leaping</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_bull_leaping_fresco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gleb Simonov, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Minoan religion has to be reconstructed without texts that can be read. The evidence comes from places of worship, figurines and offerings, and images on gold rings, sealstones, wall paintings and vessels, and almost every interpretation is debated.</p><p>Worship took place in many settings. Peak sanctuaries on mountain tops, such as Mount Juktas above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> and Petsofas above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaikastro">Palaikastro</a>, have yielded many clay figurines of people and animals and models of limbs, perhaps offered in hope of healing. Caves were sacred too, among them the Idaean Cave on Mount Ida, the Psychro cave above the Lasithi plateau and the cave of Arkalochori, with its hoard of bronze double axes. Palaces and houses had small shrines, pillar crypts and sunken rooms reached by steps, the so-called lustral basins, which may have served for purification.</p><p>Certain symbols recur: the double axe, the stylised bull’s horns that Evans called “horns of consecration”, sacred trees and pillars, birds, snakes and above all the bull. Scenes on gold rings show women and men dancing, touching sacred trees or bowing before a small figure who seems to descend from the sky, usually read as a deity appearing to worshippers. In 1903 Evans found in the Temple Repositories at Knossos faience figurines of women holding snakes, the “snake goddesses”. Evans believed in a single great mother goddess; many scholars now think of several deities, female and male, and hesitate to call a given figure goddess, priestess or worshipper. Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and stone “goddesses” that museums bought on the art market in the early 20th century are now known or suspected to be modern forgeries.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bull-leaping">Bull-leaping</a>, shown in wall paintings, on seals and in bronze, in which young athletes grasp or vault over a charging bull, may have been a ritual, a sport or both. Animal sacrifice appears on the painted limestone sarcophagus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-triada">Ayia Triada</a> of about 1400 BCE, where a trussed bull lies on a table while women make offerings and a man plays the double pipes. Whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> was ever practised is disputed: at Anemospilia, on Mount Juktas, excavators in 1979 found a building destroyed by an earthquake around 1700 BCE, with a young man’s skeleton on a platform and a bronze blade lying on it, which they read as a sacrifice interrupted by disaster; others reject that reading. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets written at Knossos after Greek speakers took over record offerings to a “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potnia">Potnia</a> of the labyrinth” and to Eleuthia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amnisos">Amnisos</a>, names that may preserve older Cretan cults.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kamares-ware"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamares-ware.jpg" alt="Kamares ware" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kamares-ware">Kamares ware</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMI_-_Kamaresvase_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Minoan craftsmen worked clay, stone, metal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and paint with exceptional skill. Potters, who adopted the fast wheel early in the Middle Minoan period, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamares-ware">Kamares ware</a> in the age of the first palaces: vessels with walls sometimes as thin as eggshell, painted in white, red and orange on a dark ground, produced chiefly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a> and prized abroad. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neopalatial-period">Neopalatial period</a> painters turned to dark designs on a light ground, with lilies and grasses and, in the Marine Style, octopuses, argonauts and seaweed spread over the whole vessel.</p><p>Wall paintings decorated palaces, villas and houses. Painted on lime plaster, often while it was still wet, they showed processions, crowds at festivals, gardens with birds and monkeys, dolphins and fish, and the famous scene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bull-leaping">bull-leaping</a> from Knossos. Most survive only as fragments, and many of the paintings seen today at Knossos and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a> Archaeological Museum were completed, sometimes boldly, by Evans’s restorers, the Swiss artist Émile Gilliéron and his son. The best-preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> wall paintings come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akrotiri-prehistoric-city">Akrotiri</a> on Thera, buried by volcanic ash: a spring landscape with swallows and lilies, two boys boxing, girls gathering saffron and a long frieze of ships sailing between harbour towns.</p><p>Engravers cut lively miniature scenes into sealstones of hard stone and the bezels of gold rings; goldsmiths mastered granulation and filigree, as in the pendant of two bees or wasps around a honeycomb from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-malia">Malia</a>; and stone carvers made vessels such as the Harvester Vase from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-triada">Ayia Triada</a>, with its procession of singing men carrying farm tools, and the bull’s-head rhyton from the Little Palace at Knossos. Builders used dressed stone for façades, wooden columns that taper downwards and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> frames within the walls, perhaps to help them withstand earthquakes.</p><p>The Minoans developed the earliest writing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Cretan hieroglyphic, used on seals and clay documents in the age of the first palaces, was joined around 1800 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a>, which survives on roughly 1,400 inscriptions, most of them short: tablets, sealings and stone offering vessels bearing dedications. Many of its signs were taken over by the Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, so some sound values can be estimated, but the language it records is unknown and the texts cannot be understood. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos-disc">Phaistos Disc</a>, found by Luigi Pernier in 1908, bears signs stamped into the clay in a spiral on both faces and remains unique and undeciphered.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vapheio"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vapheio.jpg" alt="Vapheio" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vapheio">Vapheio</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_cup_from_Vafio_1500_to_1450_BC,_NAMA_1759_080874_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> lies on the sea routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The island had little metal of its own, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> had to be imported, tin from far to the east, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, semi-precious stones and luxury goods. Egyptian stone vessels and scarabs reached Crete, and a stone lid inscribed with the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khyan">Khyan</a> was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. In return the Minoans sent fine pottery, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamares-ware">Kamares ware</a> found in Egypt and the Levant, and probably textiles, oil, wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, which leave few traces.</p><p>Records from abroad mention the Minoans. Tablets from the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, of the 18th century BCE, refer to a weapon, a vessel and cloth from Kaptara, which most scholars identify with Crete, and to tin given to a man from Kaptara. Egyptian texts speak of the Keftiu, usually taken to be the Cretans, and paintings in the Theban tombs of officials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> in the 15th century BCE, among them Senenmut and Rekhmire, show men with long locks of hair and patterned kilts bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> vessels as gifts. Minoan-style wall paintings have also been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avaris">Tell el-Dab’a</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, at Tel Kabri in present-day Israel and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, perhaps the work of Cretan painters employed by foreign rulers.</p><p>Within the Aegean, Cretan influence was so strong, especially between about 1700 and 1450 BCE, that archaeologists speak of “Minoanisation”. On Kythera, Thera, Melos, Kea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, and at Miletus on the Anatolian coast, people adopted Cretan pottery, loom weights, measures of weight, wall painting and even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a>. Whether these were Cretan colonies, trading partners or local communities imitating a prestigious culture is debated, and the answer probably differs from place to place. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> wrote in the 5th century BCE that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a> was the first to possess a navy, ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and put down piracy; the idea of a Minoan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thalassocracy">thalassocracy</a> goes back to him and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, but the archaeology suggests a network of influence rather than an empire of the sea.</p><p>The elites rising on the Greek mainland eagerly acquired Cretan goods and skills, as the Minoan-style metalwork of the shaft graves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mycenae">Mycenae</a> and the gold cups from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vapheio">Vapheio</a> show. After about 1450 BCE the relationship was reversed, when Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> took control of Knossos.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/labyrinth.jpg" alt="Labyrinth" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_silver_coin_400bc.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AlMare, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Around 1450 BCE fires destroyed the palaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-malia">Malia</a> and Zakros, villas such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-triada">Ayia Triada</a> and many towns. Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> continued as a palace, now keeping its records in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, an early form of Greek, which shows that Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> ruled there. Whether they came as invaders, took over after wars between Cretan centres, or filled a vacuum left by earthquakes or economic crisis is debated. The great eruption of Thera, one of the largest volcanic eruptions of the last few thousand years, cannot be the direct cause: it took place in the 17th or 16th century BCE, during the Late Minoan IA phase, generations before the destructions, although ash and tsunamis struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. Spyridon Marinatos, who proposed in 1939 that the eruption destroyed Minoan Crete, began excavating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akrotiri-prehistoric-city">Akrotiri</a> in 1967.</p><p>The palace of Knossos was finally destroyed in the 14th or 13th century BCE, and Chania in western Crete, ancient Kydonia, became a leading centre. As the palace states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> collapsed around 1200 BCE, Cretans moved to defensible heights such as Karphi, a mountain settlement above the Lasithi plateau excavated by John Pendlebury in 1937–1939. By about 1100 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> culture of Crete had given way to that of the Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, in which Knossos lived on as a Greek city. In eastern Crete the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eteocretans">Eteocretans</a>, the “true Cretans”, wrote a non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> in the Greek alphabet until the 3rd century BCE, perhaps a descendant of the Minoan language, though this cannot be proved.</p><p>The Greeks remembered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> Age Crete in myth: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a> the lawgiver king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a> built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a> killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a> with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> calls Crete a land of ninety cities and names Knossos as the city where Minos ruled.</p><p>Modern exploration began when Minos Kalokairinos, a merchant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a>, dug at Knossos in 1878. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> bought the site, uncovered the palace from 1900, published The Palace of Minos at Knossos (1921–1935) and rebuilt parts of it in reinforced concrete, reconstructions that still shape visitors’ impressions and remain controversial. Italian archaeologists led by Federico Halbherr and Luigi Pernier excavated Phaistos from 1900 and nearby Ayia Triada soon afterwards, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaos-platon">Nikolaos Platon</a> found the unplundered palace of Zakros in 1961. Evans’s picture of peaceful, nature-loving Minoans ruled by priest-kings and devoted to a great goddess has been qualified by finds of weapons and by debates over sacrifice, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> still keeps the Minoans’ own words beyond reach.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Inventions and discoveries credited here</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/inventions?focus=aqueduct">Aqueduct<span>c. 2000 BCE</span></a><a href="/inventions?focus=tyrian-purple">Tyrian purple<span>20th–18th century BCE</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Minoan civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s first palaces around 1900 BCE and flourished until about 1450 BCE, when Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. Known almost entirely through archaeology, its palaces, art, seafaring trade and undeciphered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> script shaped the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> world, and its memory survived in Greek myths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>.</em></p>

